## 7. Understand COUNT(\*) vs COUNT(column) with LEFT JOIN

Consider:

### users

|  id | name  |
| --: | ----- |
|   1 | Rahul |
|   2 | Priya |
|   3 | Amit  |

### orders

|  id | user_id |
| --: | ------: |
| 101 |       1 |
| 102 |       1 |
| 103 |       2 |

Query:

```sql
SELECT
    u.name,
    COUNT(*) AS count_star,
    COUNT(o.id) AS count_order_id
FROM users u
LEFT JOIN orders o
ON u.id = o.user_id
GROUP BY u.id, u.name;
```

Result:

| name  | count_star | count_order_id |
| ----- | ---------: | -------------: |
| Rahul |          2 |              2 |
| Priya |          1 |              1 |
| Amit  |          1 |              0 |

Why does Amit have `COUNT(*) = 1`?

Because LEFT JOIN creates one NULL-extended result row for Amit.

`COUNT(*)` counts rows, including that row.

`COUNT(o.id)` counts only non-NULL values of `o.id`.

Since Amit has no matching order, `o.id` is NULL.

Therefore:

```sql
COUNT(*)      -- 1
COUNT(o.id)   -- 0
```

### Best practice

When counting matching records after a LEFT JOIN, use the non-NULL primary key of the right-side table:

```sql
COUNT(o.id)
```

Do not automatically use `COUNT(*)` when you want to count matching child records.

