## 9. Be careful when joining multiple one-to-many tables

This is a particularly important problem in backend reporting queries.

Suppose we have:

### users

|  id | name  |
| --: | ----- |
|   1 | Rahul |

### orders

|  id | user_id |
| --: | ------: |
| 101 |       1 |
| 102 |       1 |

### payments

|  id | user_id | amount |
| --: | ------: | -----: |
| 201 |       1 |    500 |
| 202 |       1 |    800 |
| 203 |       1 |    300 |

Now consider:

```sql
SELECT
    u.name,
    o.id AS order_id,
    p.id AS payment_id
FROM users u
JOIN orders o
ON u.id = o.user_id
JOIN payments p
ON u.id = p.user_id;
```

### Result

| name  | order_id | payment_id |
| ----- | -------: | ---------: |
| Rahul |      101 |        201 |
| Rahul |      101 |        202 |
| Rahul |      101 |        203 |
| Rahul |      102 |        201 |
| Rahul |      102 |        202 |
| Rahul |      102 |        203 |

Why are there six rows?

Because:

- Rahul has two orders.
- Rahul has three payments.
- Each order is combined with each payment.

Therefore:

\[
2 \times 3 = 6
\]

This is called row multiplication caused by joining independent one-to-many relationships.

### Why is this dangerous?

Suppose you calculate:

```sql
SELECT
    u.name,
    COUNT(o.id) AS total_orders,
    SUM(p.amount) AS total_payments
FROM users u
JOIN orders o
ON u.id = o.user_id
JOIN payments p
ON u.id = p.user_id
GROUP BY u.id, u.name;
```

You may get:

| name  | total_orders | total_payments |
| ----- | -----------: | -------------: |
| Rahul |            6 |           3200 |

But the correct values are:

- Orders: 2
- Payments: 1,600

Why?

Each order is repeated three times, and each payment is repeated twice.

This causes incorrect aggregation.

### Solution: Aggregate independently before joining

```sql
SELECT
    u.id,
    u.name,
    COALESCE(o.total_orders, 0) AS total_orders,
    COALESCE(p.total_payments, 0) AS total_payments
FROM users u

LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT
        user_id,
        COUNT(*) AS total_orders
    FROM orders
    GROUP BY user_id
) o
ON u.id = o.user_id

LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT
        user_id,
        SUM(amount) AS total_payments
    FROM payments
    GROUP BY user_id
) p
ON u.id = p.user_id;
```

Result:

|  id | name  | total_orders | total_payments |
| --: | ----- | -----------: | -------------: |
|   1 | Rahul |            2 |           1600 |

This works because each subquery produces at most one row per user before the JOIN.

**Key lesson:** When joining multiple one-to-many relationships for reporting, consider aggregating each child table independently before joining the results.

