## 16. WHERE vs HAVING

These clauses both filter data, but they work at different logical stages.

### WHERE

Filters individual rows before grouping.

```sql
SELECT *
FROM orders
WHERE total > 1000;
```

### HAVING

Filters groups after GROUP BY.

```sql
SELECT
    user_id,
    COUNT(*) AS total_orders
FROM orders
GROUP BY user_id
HAVING COUNT(*) > 2;
```

### Comparison

| WHERE                                                                            | HAVING                             |
| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| Filters individual rows                                                          | Filters groups                     |
| Applied before GROUP BY                                                          | Applied after GROUP BY             |
| Commonly uses ordinary row conditions                                            | Commonly uses aggregate conditions |
| Cannot directly use aggregate functions at the same query level in its condition | Can use aggregate functions        |

### Example using both

Find users who have more than two completed orders.

```sql
SELECT
    user_id,
    COUNT(*) AS total_orders
FROM orders
WHERE status = 'completed'
GROUP BY user_id
HAVING COUNT(*) > 2;
```

Logical flow:

1. Filter completed orders.
2. Group by user ID.
3. Count orders per user.
4. Keep users with more than two completed orders.

