## 18. Practical JOIN best practices checklist

Before deploying a complex JOIN query, review the following.

### Correctness

- [ ] Are the tables related through the correct columns?
- [ ] Is the correct JOIN type being used?
- [ ] Are unmatched rows supposed to be preserved?
- [ ] Are NULL values handled correctly?
- [ ] Can one-to-many relationships multiply rows?
- [ ] Are aggregations affected by duplicate combinations?
- [ ] Are multiple one-to-many relationships being joined independently?

### Readability

- [ ] Are explicit JOINs used?
- [ ] Are table aliases meaningful?
- [ ] Are columns qualified with table aliases?
- [ ] Are only required columns selected?
- [ ] Are join conditions easy to understand?
- [ ] Are complex subqueries formatted clearly?

### Performance

- [ ] Are useful indexes available on join columns?
- [ ] Are unnecessary JOINs avoided?
- [ ] Are filters appropriate?
- [ ] Is the result set unnecessarily large?
- [ ] Has EXPLAIN ANALYZE been used for expensive queries?
- [ ] Are estimated and actual row counts reasonably aligned?

